Answer: void means that the method does not return any value to the calling method. Example:

```java
public static void greet() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
```

The method prints a message but does not send any value back to the caller. Therefore, its return type is void.

Answer: When a method accepts no parameters, empty parentheses are written after the method name. Example:

```java
public static void greet() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
```

The empty parentheses in greet() indicate that the method accepts no input.

Answer: Every method, including the main() method, must be written inside a class boundary. Example:

```java
class PrintingApp {
public static void greet() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
}
```

A method cannot be declared independently outside a class.

Answer: An empty main() method contains no executable statements. Even if another method is defined in the class, it will not execute automatically. It must be called explicitly. Example:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
}
```

This method produces no output.

Answer: A method definition contains the statements that perform the task.

```java
public static void greet() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
```

A method call requests the execution of that method.

```java
greet();
```

The definition specifies what the method does, while the call starts its execution.

